WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.360
Film Courage: When you were in film school what were
some of the things the teachers did that

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:07.109
you said you know what this really works
for me and if I ever become a teacher I

00:00:07.109 --> 00:00:10.980
want to emulate that and then on the
reverse that it sort of shut you down?

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:14.639
Aleem Hossain, filmmaker and AR/VR creator, assistant professor in media arts and culture at Occidental College: One of the most important things that
happen to me in film school was I had a

00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:20.699
professor who introduced me to the idea
that art could be about process not

00:00:20.699 --> 00:00:25.680
result and he showed a series of
experimental films that honestly even

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:29.699
narrative films that were built around a
way of working or an approach or a

00:00:29.699 --> 00:00:34.260
system and the idea that you could do
that and then be open to the result and

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:37.710
I don't make films that are as
experimental as that but that overall

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:41.280
idea it just changed my entire way of
thinking and I'm so grateful that

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.940
someone introduced me to that idea you
know we live in a media environment

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:48.539
where I think we're very result oriented
you know how did how much money this

00:00:48.539 --> 00:00:53.489
film make what people think of it or the
reviews that kind of stuff and as an

00:00:53.489 --> 00:00:58.500
artist I really realized I have to be
happy with the process to and to explain

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:02.640
have a journey in the process so that
was super important and I definitely

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:06.420
also learned that I had I had professors
who were super interested in engaging

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:12.810
what was new and risky and exciting I
did also encounter though a certain sort

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:16.200
of skepticism towards new technology and
I actually really promised myself that I

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.310
wasn't going to be someone who when the
new thing came along I would grumble

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:25.200
about it and ignore it I I remember some
minority of them but a few professors

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.320
that are like really being against
digital cameras for example they just

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.560
didn't like them and they weren't that
great at the time but it was just so

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:34.409
clear to me they would obviously get
better you know and here we are years

00:01:34.409 --> 00:01:38.549
later and I love film but we have great
digital cameras too and so I actually

00:01:38.549 --> 00:01:42.689
had that experience literally happen to
the professor several years ago I got

00:01:42.689 --> 00:01:48.180
access to an oculus rift Developer Kit
put on the headset and my instant

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:52.770
reaction was like look like I don't like
this every tool of cinema is gone to me

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:56.070
I don't have a frame I don't have
editing you know I don't know if

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:59.570
lighting that much and I was like I
don't wanna do anything do with that and

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:05.490
thankfully in that moment I was like
wait wait you're being that old group of

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:10.200
professors do that and so instead I
threw myself into it and I've done a lot

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:16.260
of work in VR now and honestly I spent
long amount of time not liking it but

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:21.180
still doing it but I was just convinced
there's gotta be something to it and now

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:24.900
I'm at the point where as I've now fully
understood it to be different from film

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:30.210
I do like some things about it but for
sure I had to overcome a lot of my like

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:35.580
grumpy old dinosaur filmmaker sort of
attitude to sort of see what was good

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:43.170
about it what about that your work is
going to invoke either not not dislike

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:45.720
but anger and some people like I'm
thinking of Chris burden I don't know if

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.810
you saw that's not you know you did the
LACMA lights and yeah he did a lot of

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:52.920
experimental stuff yeah that made people
angry you know and and he's not the only

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:57.140
artist but and being okay with that I

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:03.740
like the idea of movies that provoke
questions and provoke a response and I'm

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:08.700
perfectly okay people not liking the
movies you know if someone yeah it isn't

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:12.630
their cup of tea if they have an
objection to it I will say like I don't

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:15.720
know that I'm a place in my life where I
want to make a movie that I think will

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:20.070
be consciously hurtful to anyone and I
do think about that for sure like

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:27.240
there's a line I think between provoking
deep questioning or challenging dominant

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:31.530
ideas and then there's another level
which is like yeah I but what I don't

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:34.950
want to do is be hateful or hurtful and
I don't think that I am but like I do

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:39.690
think about that so I'm excited for
people to debate the movies and I'm

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:44.910
excited for people to sort of engage
with them and I'm even okay with people

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:47.910
who are like you know I didn't really
like it I think that that to me that

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:51.900
doesn't impact my artistic choices and I
like filmmakers who do that - there are

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:54.870
filmmakers who I respect whose movie
that actually optically like you know

00:03:54.870 --> 00:04:00.300
and and I'm glad they exist I think for
me the only thing that I'm not really

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:05.430
seeing is where I fit we cross the line
between you know expressing alternate

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:12.000
ideas to like essentially being you know
purple and now - Debbie do you prepare

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.540
your students for that because it's like
I'm gonna date myself again Gen X we

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:19.590
didn't have we had party lines you can
call party line talk to me well you

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:23.010
didn't have social media where you could
say I hated this I hate the filmmaker

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:28.470
you're awful and now that's a very real
of our culture even if it's just someone

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:32.490
that just wants to invoke an emotion in
the filmmaker yeah I mean I think that

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:36.030
the the social media environmentally the
hardest for those of us that didn't come

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:40.710
of age in it like you know I when I
started a filmmaker as a filmmaker it

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:43.950
was just beginning right the Silicon
Valley explosion was just happening and

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:48.090
so for me like yeah it is a lot have to
definitely sort of remind myself anytime

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:51.750
I go on Twitter there is a chance that
someone is just gonna tell me they hate

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:54.330
my movie you know actually doesn't
happen that often it's like oh maybe

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:58.770
only one or two times but yeah for sure
you have to balance if emotion when you

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:02.250
hear that but when I watch my students I
think the truth is it's less that

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:04.860
they're better at navigating that but
more that that's just a fact of life for

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:09.840
them in all aspects of life that like
everything they you know that they do is

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:13.290
in some ways always you know subject to
a lot of different commentary or

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:17.010
judgment or ideas and so I think I don't
see them as like particularly sort of

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:20.070
thinking about that being unique to
their filmmaking experience as it is

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:24.120
just reflective of their life experience
interesting so maybe older people myself

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:29.370
maybe part of them we're having a hard
time interesting okay well I'm the I

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:31.970
feel that way

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:41.450
you

